Quoting  EMK <noneofyourbusiness@columbus.rr.com>:
>After my first excursion into the game and finding that I was dead of 
>starvation within 10 minutes (real time), I figure that I need to get a 
>better handle on how time works in this game.  For instance, I get that 
>moving one space is considered a "turn," but how long is that exactly?

That is unspecified.

>And what other actions/commands take a "turn" to complete?
>Which don't take any time at all?  Are there any that take some
>fraction of a turn?

An action that does not do anything in-game (for example, checking your
inventory or list of known spells) does not take time.

Most actions which do do something in-game take one turn. An important
exception is using a stethoscope, which is free if not repeated.

Some actions take multiple turns, but are interrupted by hostiles heaving
into view. An example is eating.

A few actions are not interruptible. For example, getting into or out of
armour.

>1. Step-n10s to search each space 10 times before moving on to the next 
>space or 2. Step-s to search each space once before giving the whole area a 
>second sweep.  Which would be more efficient game-time-wise? 

The former.
